<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Charles Babbage</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1791">1791</a>–<a href="/years/1871">1871</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Charles Babbage was an English mathematician, philosopher, inventor and mechanical engineer who originated the concept of a digital programmable computer. Most likely born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> in 1791, he was Lucasian Professor of Mathematics at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridge">Cambridge</a> from 1828 to 1839. From 1822 he worked on the difference engine, made to compute values of polynomial functions, and later designed the analytical engine, to be programmed with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punched-card">punched cards</a> as on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jacquard-machine">Jacquard loom</a>. He wrote programs for it from 1837 to 1840, but died at Marylebone in 1871 before his engines were completed; a functioning difference engine built from his plans in 1991 showed that the design would have worked.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/charles-babbage.jpg" alt="Charles Babbage" width="330" height="516" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Charles_Babbage_-_1860.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Babbage's birthplace is disputed, but the Oxford Dictionary of National Biography holds that he was most likely born at 44 Crosby Row, Walworth Road, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>. His obituary in The Times gave 26 December 1792, but a nephew wrote that he was born a year earlier, and the parish register records his baptism on 6 January 1792, which supports 1791. He arrived at Trinity College, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridge">Cambridge</a>, in October 1810, already self-taught in parts of contemporary mathematics, and was disappointed by the instruction there. In 1812 he, John Herschel, George Peacock and friends formed the Analytical Society.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>He was elected a Fellow of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-society">Royal Society</a> in 1816. He helped found the Astronomical Society of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, later the Royal Astronomical Society, in 1820 and won its Gold Medal in 1824 for his invention of an engine for calculating tables. As Lucasian Professor of Mathematics at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridge">Cambridge</a> he was inattentive to teaching and never gave a lecture. His Reflections on the Decline of Science in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> (1830) led to the formation in 1831 of the British Association for the Advancement of Science, and his On the Economy of Machinery and Manufactures (1832) was an influential early work of operational research.</p><p>In 1838 he invented the cow-catcher, the metal frame on the front of locomotives that clears the track. He made notable advances in cryptography that stayed unknown long after his death, and campaigned against street music.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Babbage began in 1822 with the difference engine, made to compute polynomial values by the method of finite differences. Joseph Clement built a prototype from 1823, but he and Babbage fell out over costs around 1831. After the attempt fell through Babbage designed the more complex <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/analytical-engine">Analytical Engine</a>, which marks the transition from mechanised arithmetic to general-purpose computation. Its architecture resembled a modern computer, with separate program and data memory, conditional jumps and a separate input and output unit.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ada-lovelace">Ada Lovelace</a> is credited with an algorithm for calculating Bernoulli numbers on it, though some scholars dispute how far the ideas were hers. Babbage received about £17,000 in government funding before the Treasury lost confidence in him, and clashes with George Biddell Airy also hampered the work. He died at Marylebone on 18 October 1871, aged 79, having declined both a knighthood and a baronetcy.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Charles Babbage” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
